WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.810
Understand the customer: needs, listening and trust.

2
00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.440
This is the video for module 1 of Customer Relations and Sales.

3
00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:14.490
Customer relations means how you treat people before, during and after they buy.

4
00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:21.890
Sales means helping a person decide to buy something that is right for her. The two belong together.

5
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:26.420
A trader who only pushes sales gets one purchase.

6
00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:34.500
A trader who builds relationships gets the same customer back again and again, plus the people she recommends.

7
00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:42.280
Every product has features. A feature is a fact about it. For example, this shea butter is unrefined.

8
00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:50.850
A benefit is what that feature does for the person. Unrefined shea butter keeps its natural moisturising quality.

9
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:57.710
A need is the real reason she is shopping. Perhaps her child has dry skin in harmattan.

10
00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:02.830
People buy to meet needs. Features only matter when you connect them to that need.

11
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:14.430
Most buying decisions come down to four questions, even if she never says them aloud. First, need. Without it, good price and trust will not produce a sale.

12
00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:22.210
Second, value. Is the price fair compared with other options she knows? Price is only one part of value.

13
00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:28.150
Third, trust. Will you deliver what you promised, and help her if something goes wrong?

14
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.310
Fourth, convenience. Can she buy, pay and receive it easily?

15
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:38.840
Many customers pay a little more to a seller who answers quickly and delivers on time.

16
00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:50.970
Here is why this matters in money. Ngozi sells pap and moi moi from a stall in Enugu. An office worker, Chika, buys breakfast from her on most working days.

17
00:01:52.220 --> 00:02:01.820
Chika spends about 1,200 naira each visit. She comes about 4 days a week, so that is about 4,800 naira a week.

18
00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:09.210
Over about 48 working weeks, that is about 230,400 naira in a year.

19
00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:18.570
If Chika also brings two colleagues who each spend half as much, that adds about 230,400 naira more.

20
00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:25.150
One badly handled day, with cold food, a rude reply or wrong change, could lose all of this.

21
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:32.630
Active listening means paying full attention to understand her, not just waiting for your turn to speak.

22
00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:41.470
Habit one, attend. Put your phone down and face her. On WhatsApp, read the whole message and listen to the whole voice note before you reply.

23
00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:53.280
Habit two, ask. Use open questions that begin with what, how, who or when. Instead of asking, do you want a cake, ask, what kind of celebration are you planning?

24
00:02:54.530 --> 00:03:03.870
Habit three, reflect. Repeat back the key point, including feelings. So you have guests coming on Saturday and you are worried the food will not be enough.

25
00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:10.310
Habit four, summarise and confirm. Gather the details and ask, is that correct?

26
00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:18.210
The common mistake is talking about your products before you understand the customer. Ask first, talk second.

27
00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:27.740
Trust also means handling money safely. Fake transfer alerts are a common trick. Screenshots and text messages can be faked.

28
00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:33.130
When a customer says she has paid, open your own bank or POS app.

29
00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.070
Check your balance and history.

30
00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:46.470
Only when the amount and name match do you release the goods. You can say politely, thank you Ma, let me just confirm on my app, it takes one minute.

31
00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:56.320
Be careful when a buyer rushes you or sends a screenshot instead of waiting. Never skip the bank app check, however friendly the customer is.

32
00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:10.370
State the full price and terms in writing before she pays. Her number and address are personal data, so collect only what you need, ask before adding her to a broadcast list, and never share her number.

33
00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:17.260
Meet buyers in busy public places. And if there is a delay, tell her before the deadline, not after.

34
00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:29.200
To remember. People buy to meet needs. They weigh need, value, trust and convenience. Listen before you recommend. Only money in your own app is proof of payment.

35
00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:34.370
And unhappy customers usually leave quietly, so notice who has gone quiet.

36
00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:49.750
Your turn. Map one product from feature to benefit to need, and estimate one regular customer's yearly value. Write five open questions and practise with a friend. Then write your terms message and payment check.

37
00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.040
Continue with the lessons below.
